# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# The interface of fetchers: a process that brings the
# content of images into the staging directories of a store.  Every fetcher
# serves it; nothing in it names a kind of fetcher.  Generated from the Rust
# types (crates/content/src/fetcher/fetch.rs and crates/content/src/serve_fetch.rs)
# by the test `serve_fetch::tests::the_interface_file_is_current`:
# VELARX_WRITE_INTERFACES=1 cargo test -p velarx-content --lib serve_fetch writes it.
#
# ONE FETCHER.  A fetcher answers only for itself: what it does not have is
# unavailable, what it tried and could not bring is failed, with a class that says what to do next.  It knows nothing
# of other fetchers; the engine walks its ordered list of them, and is the
# one that asks the next.  Every item of a call is answered exactly once.
#
# THE STAGING DIRECTORY OF A LAYER.  A directory open for reading (not O_PATH) on
# the file system of the store, made by the engine, one per layer.  A fetcher
# resolves every path beneath it with openat2(RESOLVE_BENEATH |
# RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS | RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS | RESOLVE_NO_XDEV) and uses
# incoming/ (files with no name), files/ab/cd... (the files of the layer, named
# after fs-verity was enabled, by the digest the table of contents gives), and
# toc.claim and tarsplit (only for a layer without a table of contents).
# A built-in fetcher may also be confined, at its start, to the staging
# root of its store; that is its sandbox, not part of this interface: an
# external fetcher reaches a layer's staging only through the descriptor
# of `Layer`.
#
# THE OBJECT CONTRACT.  A file is written into a file with no name, each chunk
# checked against the sha256 the table of contents gives for it; the file is
# closed for writing; fs-verity is enabled on it (when the scope says verity);
# its mode is 0444; then it is given its name with linkat, never replacing.  A
# name that exists is success.  A fetcher syncs nothing, removes no name,
# writes nowhere else, and never reads the store: the chunks of the store it
# may take (`reuse` of Fetch, only for a fetcher that announces the word
# reuse) come in one file the engine made of exactly those bytes, read-only.
#
# UNPACK.  The origin reads a layer with no table of contents whole, and its
# regular files are handed over, not named: each is a descriptor of a reply of
# `Unpack`, a complete file with no name (a small one may be a file in memory)
# that has fs-verity when the scope says verity.  Then it gives `toc.claim` and
# `tarsplit` their names in the staging directory.  A file of two names is handed
# over once.
#
# HANDOVER.  A fetcher that announces the word handover and is called with
# `handover` may answer for a small file, written in memory, with a `staged`
# event that has `files`: for each of its `objects` the descriptor of the file, a
# complete file with no name, instead of a name in the staging directory.  A file
# that is not small, or that needs fs-verity, is named as before.
#
# TABLES.  A zstd:chunked layer whose descriptor has the annotations
# `table-position` and `table-checksum` is pulled by its binary table, not by
# its JSON table of contents: only a fetcher that announces the word table is
# asked for it, and the table is what `Probe` carries and `Layer` gives.
#
# TRUST.  A fetcher is not trusted.  A name it made is a claim; the engine opens
# it and takes the kernel's measure.  A file it hands over is a claim too: the
# engine copies it, or takes the kernel's measure of it, and knows it by the
# digest of the bytes it read.  What it returns with GetFile (a manifest),
# GetBlob and Probe is hashed by the engine before it is used,
# and the signatures of GetFile are judged by the engine's policy.
#
# CAPABILITIES.  What a fetcher can do is a word of capabilities in GetInfo; a
# client ignores the words it does not know; what it cannot do without is a
# word of requires in Open, refused with NotSupported when unknown.  The
# interface grows only so: a new parameter or reply field is optional, a new
# value of an enum of a call is sent only to a fetcher that announces its word,
# and a value of an enum of a reply that a client does not know is read as
# the most general one (other, none, unsupported) or ignored (an event).
#
# DESCRIPTORS.  A descriptor is a field of type `int` (or `?int` when it may
# be absent), named after what it is, whose value is its position among the
# descriptors of its message; at most 16 for a message.  Nothing is
# counted and nothing is implied by order.  A sealed memory file carries no
# length: the receiver checks the seals and applies the interface's maximum.
#
# REPLIES.  A method called with more answers one record a reply; its last
# reply carries no record and has continues false (`Fetch`: no event;
# `Probe`: no probed; `Unpack`: neither bytes nor files).  A server whose connection is closed sends nothing
# more.  `progress` carries `objects` (exactly one) and `bytes`, the cumulative
# bytes of that object moved by this call; with no object, the cumulative
# bytes of the blob of the layer; one with no object and no bytes is a beat.
# Every other method has one reply.
#
# LIVENESS.  `Open` carries `idle_ms`.  A call with more sends a reply (a
# progress when it has nothing else) at least every `idle_ms / 2`; a call with
# one reply answers within `idle_ms`.  Otherwise the engine closes the
# connection and records a transient failure, `silent for N ms`, for that
# party.
# Who may call: a peer of the same user or root (SO_PEERCRED); any
# accepted peer may make every call.

interface org.velarx.Fetcher

# What a `GetFile` asks for.
type FileKind (
	# A manifest or an index, by tag or digest
	manifest,
	# The startup profile of an image
	profile,
	# The signatures of a manifest or a tag
	signatures
)

# How much a request is waited for.  It orders a fetcher's work.
type Urgency (
	# The rest of an image that already runs
	background,
	# A guess
	ahead,
	# A command waits for the image
	wait,
	# A process of a container is blocked on it
	on_demand
)

# A blob of the image, as the manifest the engine hashed describes it.
type Blob (
	digest: string,
	size: int,
	media_type: string,
	# The annotations of its descriptor in the manifest (where a table of
	# contents is, for zstd:chunked and eStargz)
	annotations: ?[string]string
)

# A chunk the store of the engine holds: `length` bytes at `offset` of
# the descriptor `fd` of the message (a file with no name the engine made
# of the chunks it hints, read-only; never a file of the store).  A
# fetcher may take a chunk of a file it writes whose sha256 is `chunk`
# from there instead of fetching it, after it checked those bytes against
# `chunk` as it checks every chunk; it may also ignore the hint.
type Reuse (chunk: string, fd: int, offset: int, length: int)

# How the table of contents of a layer comes.
type TocSource (
	# The blob has one (zstd:chunked, eStargz): the reply carries it (the
	# table, then the tar-split)
	carried,
	# It has none: the layer is unpacked whole (`Unpack`), which
	# gives the files, a table and a tar-split in one pass
	unpack,
	# This fetcher cannot bring the layer at all (also what a value this
	# side does not know is read as)
	none
)

# What a `Probe` asks a fetcher to carry for a layer with a table of
# contents.  A fetcher may carry more than it is asked for.
type Parts (
	# The table of contents: a lazy start, which does not need the
	# tar-split
	toc,
	# The tar-split: the completion of a lazy pull
	tarsplit,
	# Both (also what a value this side does not know is read as)
	all
)

# Why a layer cannot be served on demand (the Fetcher's answer to `Probe`,
# and the engine's `ImageNotLazy`).
type NotLazy (
	# The blob has no table of contents (gzip, plain zstd)
	no_toc,
	# The origin does not serve ranges of the blob
	no_ranges,
	# This fetcher cannot reach the layer
	unreachable,
	# A compression or media type the fetcher does not read (also what a
	# value this side does not know is read as)
	unsupported
)

# What kind of failure, for the party that reads it: what to do next.
type FailureClass (
	# This party does not have the item or cannot serve it now; nothing
	# is wrong: the engine asks the next one
	unavailable,
	# It tried and failed in a way that may pass (a timeout, a cut
	# connection, a server error): the engine asks the next one; the
	# call may be tried again
	transient,
	# What it brought, or what it was handed, is not what it claims (a
	# digest, a size, a table of contents): the operation fails and names
	# this party; nobody else is asked
	invalid,
	# The origin says the item does not exist; from a party that is not
	# the origin it is read as `unavailable`
	not_found,
	# Credentials missing or refused
	unauthorized,
	# The credentials were taken and do not allow it
	forbidden,
	# No space where the bytes go
	no_space,
	# It tried and failed for good (also what a value this side does not
	# know is read as)
	failed
)

# Why something failed.
type Failure (class: FailureClass, reason: string)

# What an event of a `Fetch` tells.
type EventKind (
	# `objects` have their names in `files/` of the layer's staging, or,
	# with `files`, are the descriptors of the reply
	staged,
	# This fetcher does not have `objects`
	unavailable,
	# It tried and failed for `objects`: `failure`
	failed,
	# `bytes` of the one object of `objects` are written so far by this
	# call (of the blob of the layer, for a call with no objects)
	progress,
	# (what an event this side does not know is read as: ignored)
	unknown
)

# What an event of a `Fetch` carries.
type FetchEvent (
	kind: EventKind,
	# Positions in the call
	objects: []int,
	failure: ?Failure,
	bytes: ?int,
	# With `staged`: the object at each position of `objects` is the
	# complete file with no name at this position among the descriptors of
	# the reply (a call that asked for `handover`)
	files: ?[]int
)

# What this fetcher can do for one layer of a `Probe`.
type Probed (
	# The position of the layer in the call
	layer: int,
	toc: TocSource,
	# Its files can be fetched one by one, on demand
	on_demand: bool,
	# Why not, when `on_demand` is false
	reason: ?NotLazy,
	# With `toc` carried: the table of contents and the tar-split, as the
	# `parts` of the probe ask, sealed memory files among the descriptors of
	# this reply (a layer whose table of contents has `"version": 2` has no
	# tar-split).  For a layer with no
	# table of contents, with `no_toc`: the index another node recorded, a
	# claim the engine replays and checks
	toc_file: ?int,
	tarsplit_file: ?int
)

# Who the fetcher is and what it can do.
method GetInfo() -> (
	name: string,
	# The version of the package
	version: string,
	# The version of the protocol (1)
	proto: int,
	# Words; a client ignores those it does not know
	capabilities: []string,
	# Named limits (`max_objects`, `max_wants`, ...); a client ignores those it does not know
	limits: [string]int
)

# Give the connection its scope; once, before anything but
# `GetInfo`.  `source`: what the origin's transport names
# (`registry/name`, `oci:PATH`, `oci-archive:`, `SCHEME://...`);
# absent for a fetcher that is not the origin.  `tls_verify`
# (default yes) is how a network origin is reached.  `target`: the
# origin the client opened, for an origin that is a path (a
# directory, or a file open for reading).  `access`: the
# credentials, a sealed memory file.  `idle_ms`: the most this
# connection's calls stay silent.
method Open(verity: bool, source: ?string, tls_verify: ?bool, target: ?int, access: ?int, idle_ms: int, requires: []string) -> ()

# Another connection with the scope of this one: the reply carries
# its descriptor.
method Connect() -> ()

# Tell this fetcher of a layer: `staging` its staging directory,
# `toc` its table of contents (JSON, or the binary table of a layer
# that has one, compressed with zstd, a sealed
# memory file).  `Layer` again replaces it; the calls in flight on the
# one before end as with `Forget`.  A scope keeps at most 1024 layers
# (`Busy`).
method Layer(layer: string, staging: int, blob: ?Blob, toc: ?int) -> ()

# Forget the layer: its calls end, later ones are `Refused`, and what
# the fetcher kept for its blob is let go (of a layer that was only
# probed too).
method Forget(layer: string) -> ()

# A small file of the origin, at most `max` bytes, in `file`, a
# sealed memory file in the reply.  `kind` says which: the manifest
# or index `reference` names (a tag or a digest), with its media
# type, which the engine hashes; the startup profile of the image
# `reference` (its id), digests of files, one a line, in the order
# the image read them; the signatures of the manifest or tag
# `reference` names (a `repository:tag`, or a manifest digest), a
# JSON array of records, `{"digest": "sha256:...", "signatures":
# ["<base64 of a signature blob>", ...]}`, which the engine judges
# itself.  What the fetcher has none of is `Failed` of class
# `unavailable`.
method GetFile(kind: FileKind, reference: string, max: int) -> (file: int, media_type: ?string)

# A blob, its bytes written into `file`, a file with no name the
# engine made, open for writing, among the descriptors of the call:
# exactly `blob.size` bytes of the digest, which the fetcher checks as it
# writes and the engine checks again.  Nothing holds the blob in
# memory.
method GetBlob(blob: Blob, file: int) -> ()

# What can this fetcher do for `layers`?  Called with `more`: one
# `probed` a reply, in any order, then a last reply without one.
# `again`: the index this fetcher carried for a layer without a
# table of contents in the last `Probe` of the scope was wrong (its
# replay failed): it carries one from another of its providers, or
# none.  `parts`: what of a table of contents to carry (all of it
# when not given).
method Probe(layers: []Blob, again: ?bool, parts: ?Parts) -> (probed: ?Probed)

# Read the whole blob of `layer` (one with no table of contents) and
# hand over its files, then give `toc.claim` and `tarsplit` names in
# its staging.  Called with more: replies with `bytes` (of the blob,
# read so far) or `files` (complete files of the layer: the
# position of a descriptor of the reply each, regular files with no
# name, in memory when small, with fs-verity when the scope says
# verity), then a last reply with neither once the names are given.
# A failure is the error `Failed`.
method Unpack(layer: string, urgency: Urgency) -> (
	bytes: ?int,
	# Complete files of the layer, each the position of a descriptor of the
	# reply
	files: ?[]int
)

# Bring files of `layer` into its staging directory, by the object
# contract.  Called with more.  Each object is in exactly one of `staged`,
# `unavailable`, `failed`; the last reply has no event.  Descriptors:
# the file of the chunks of the store `reuse` names, by `fd` (the
# engine made it of exactly those bytes; only for a fetcher that
# announces `reuse`).  With `handover`, a fetcher that announces the
# word hands over the files it keeps in memory as descriptors of
# its `staged` events, and names the others.
method Fetch(layer: string, objects: []string, urgency: Urgency, reuse: ?[]Reuse, handover: ?bool) -> (event: ?FetchEvent)

error InvalidArgument (reason: string)

# A call before `Open`, an `Open` twice
error NotScoped (reason: string)

# A call this connection may not make; a file that is not of the layer;
# a layer that was not told (`Layer`) or was forgotten (`Forget`)
error Refused (reason: string)

# A word of `requires` that is not known
error NotSupported (feature: string)

# The answer of a `GetFile` is larger
# than `max`
error TooLarge (max: int)

# No connection for this client now (its limit): asked again after
# one of its own calls ended, not a fetcher that failed
error Busy (reason: string)

# Not a directory of a file system that has what the scope asks for
error BadStaging (reason: string)

# The call failed: what kind, and why
error Failed (failure: Failure)
